::U6L4Q01::Appropriate prescription use includes {=the right person, medicine, dose, time/route, and purpose ~sharing with a friend ~changing the dose independently ~using an unknown pill}

::U6L4Q02::Prescription drug misuse is {=use in a way not directed ~every prescribed use ~safe disposal ~pharmacist guidance}

::U6L4Q03::Opioids can dangerously slow {=breathing ~packaging ~hearing only ~advertising}

::U6L4Q04::A stimulant generally {=increases central nervous system activity ~always slows breathing ~reverses every depressant ~is harmless if prescribed to someone}

::U6L4Q05::An OTC medicine is {=available without a prescription but still requires label-safe use ~risk-free at any dose ~safe to mix with anything ~only an herbal product}

::U6L4Q06::A drug interaction occurs when {=one substance changes another's effect ~a pill changes color ~a prescription is filled ~a label is read}

::U6L4Q07::Polysubstance use means {=using multiple substances ~using one prescribed dose ~safe storage ~calling Poison Control}

::U6L4Q08::Fentanyl is {=a very potent synthetic opioid ~an OTC vitamin ~a stimulant antidote ~a packaging material}

::U6L4Q09::A counterfeit pill is {=a fake pill made to resemble legitimate medicine ~any generic medicine from a pharmacy ~a safely disposed pill ~an empty capsule}

::U6L4Q10::Naloxone can {=temporarily reverse opioid overdose but not replace emergency care ~identify every drug ~reverse stimulant toxicity ~guarantee permanent recovery}

::U6L4Q11::A friend offers a prescribed stimulant. The safest choice is to {=refuse because it was not prescribed to you ~take half ~save it for later ~mix it with caffeine}

::U6L4Q12::Why can alcohol plus a depressant be dangerous? {=Their slowing effects may compound, including breathing suppression ~They always cancel each other ~Alcohol verifies the dose ~The mixture becomes a stimulant}

::U6L4Q13::Why does appearance not verify a pill's contents? {=Counterfeit pills can copy color, shape, and imprint ~All pills look different ~Only pharmacies use imprints ~Potency is visible}

::U6L4Q14::Which source is safest for a prescribed medicine? {=A licensed pharmacy dispensing it to the named patient ~A social-media seller ~A friend's backpack ~An unlabeled container}

::U6L4Q15::An opioid overdose may include {=unresponsiveness and slow or stopped breathing ~improved coordination ~faster reading ~mild hunger only}

::U6L4Q16::The first response to suspected overdose is to {=call emergency services immediately ~wait for certainty ~make the person walk ~give food}

::U6L4Q17::After naloxone is given, a bystander should {=continue emergency response and stay until help arrives ~cancel the emergency call ~leave the person alone ~force a drink}

::U6L4Q18::Which action should be avoided during suspected overdose? {=Forcing food or drink ~Following dispatcher directions ~Monitoring breathing ~Calling emergency services}

::U6L4Q19::Gurgling with unresponsiveness is {=an emergency warning sign ~proof the person is sleeping safely ~a reason to delay ~a request for food}

::U6L4Q20::A cold shower is inappropriate because it {=does not reverse overdose and can delay effective care ~always increases breathing ~identifies fentanyl ~replaces naloxone}

::U6L4Q21::Secure medication storage helps {=prevent unauthorized access ~increase potency ~remove all interactions ~replace disposal}

::U6L4Q22::The best disposal option for many unused medicines is {=an authorized take-back program ~giving them to a friend ~leaving them unlocked ~mixing them into food for use}

::U6L4Q23::Stigma can increase harm by {=discouraging help-seeking ~verifying a pill ~reversing overdose ~preventing dependence}

::U6L4Q24::A stimulant and depressant together {=do not safely balance and can create unpredictable stress ~always cancel effects ~become an OTC drug ~prevent toxicity}

::U6L4Q25::Which evidence supports refusing an online pill? {=The source and contents cannot be verified ~The photo looks professional ~The seller has followers ~The pill has a common color}

::U6L4Q26::An awake person asks whether an OTC dose is safe. The best response is {=check the label and consult a pharmacist/provider or Poison Control as appropriate ~guess from pill size ~double the dose ~use someone else's directions}

::U6L4Q27::Which chain is accurate? {=opioid exposure → respiratory depression → oxygen loss → emergency ~opioid exposure → guaranteed alertness → safe driving ~counterfeit pill → verified contents → no risk ~naloxone → no need for care}

::U6L4Q28::Why must an unresponsive person not be left alone? {=Breathing can worsen and continuous monitoring/help are needed ~Privacy is more important ~Walking is required ~Food must be prepared}

::U6L4Q29::Which response demonstrates evidence-based reasoning? {=Abnormal breathing and unresponsiveness threaten life, so emergency action cannot wait for certainty ~The pill is small, so wait ~The person is young, so overdose is impossible ~A friend says it is safe, so leave}

::U6L4Q30::Good Samaritan protections are best described as {=laws that may encourage emergency calls and vary by jurisdiction ~a guarantee that no law applies ~permission to share prescriptions ~a substitute for emergency services}
